I updated my patches today for 3.4.2. Since the RNG issue has been
solved for Linux and some other bugfixes became obsolete this is likely
to concern you only if you are interested in "nested looting" or the
ranger ammo patch, both of which affect gameplay (but do not fix any
bugs). I actually fixed an embarassing probabilities bug I found during
inspection of nloot-342.diff, so now it works (hopefully, since I have
tested it) exactly like the step-by-step version in 100% of the cases.

Nevertheless, the "RNG bug" itself still remains. Even though Nethack
now uses random() for Linux, which uses a non-linear method according to
specification, there are systems on which rand() or lrand48() would be
used. The former function is an intentionally underspecified one,
leaving the PRNG method open, the latter always uses LCM.

Thus, there will be cases where nethack is working on a system with less
random lower bits, leading to 1st level/character generation problems.
In such cases, one should either use nethack's own random package or, if
a system random() is available, manually tweak the game to use it.

The real solution to this is to let [os]conf.h (which defines Rand() and
is likely to know of the quality of pseudo-random numbers on the system)
inform rnd.c whether it needs to take some action or can use any
Rand()-supplied bit-sequence modulo powers of 2 as is.

I am inclined to think that even something as simple as bit-reversal or
my own nonFPA formula from the old patch would work adequately for
nethack. The DevTeam does not care about my proposal however, perhaps
because they think that too few users are affected.

